(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-11-04"] = {"mth-11-04-m01":{"idea":"A parabola is defined by one fixed point and one fixed line. The point is the focus. The line is the directrix.","steps":["Take a point P that moves in the plane.","Let S be the focus and PM the perpendicular distance from P to the directrix.","For a parabola, SP = PM at every position of P.","So the ratio SP/PM is 1."],"others":"A second fixed point would give an ellipse or a hyperbola, depending on the sum or difference of distances. Twice the distance gives a hyperbola (ratio 2). Half the distance gives an ellipse (ratio 1/2).","trap":"Parabola: distance to focus equals distance to directrix.","answer":"equal to its distance from a fixed line (the directrix)"},"mth-11-04-m02":{"idea":"Every conic has an eccentricity e = SP/PM. Its value tells which conic we have.","steps":["e = 1 gives a parabola.","e less than 1 gives an ellipse.","e greater than 1 gives a hyperbola.","So an ellipse has e less than 1."],"others":"e = 0 is only true for a circle, which is a special ellipse, not every ellipse. e = 1 is the parabola. e greater than 1 is the hyperbola.","trap":"Ellipse is round: e between 0 and 1.","answer":"e less than 1"},"mth-11-04-m03":{"idea":"The standard parabola has its vertex at the origin. Its axis and opening direction decide which variable is squared.","steps":["The axis is the x axis, so the curve is symmetric about it.","Then y appears as y², which is symmetric in y.","The focus is (a, 0) and the directrix is x = −a.","So the equation is y² = 4ax, which opens to the right for a > 0."],"others":"x² = 4ay has its axis along the y axis. y = 4ax² is also a parabola with a vertical axis. y² = −4ax opens to the left.","trap":"Squared variable = the variable perpendicular to the axis.","answer":"y² = 4ax"},"mth-11-04-m04":{"idea":"For y² = 4ax, the focus and directrix lie on opposite sides of the vertex at distance a.","steps":["Compare y² = 4ax with the given equation to find a.","The focus is a units right of the vertex: (a, 0).","The directrix is a units left of the vertex: x = −a.","Example: y² = 8x gives a = 2, focus (2, 0), directrix x = −2."],"others":"(−a, 0) with x = a is for the parabola that opens to the left. (0, a ) with y = −a is for the parabola x² = 4ay. A directrix at x = a through the focus is not possible.","trap":"The focus is inside the curve, and the directrix is outside.","answer":"(a, 0) and x = −a"},"mth-11-04-m05":{"idea":"For a point on a parabola, the distance to the focus equals its distance to the directrix. This is easy to compute.","steps":["The directrix is x = −a.","The distance of P(x₁, y₁) from it is x₁ − (−a ) = x₁ + a.","By definition SP = PM, so SP = x₁ + a.","Example: (2, 4) on y² = 8x gives 2 + 2 = 4."],"others":"x₁ + 2a and y₁ + a come from wrong distances. x₁ − a is the distance from a line through the focus, not from the directrix.","trap":"Add a, because the directrix is on the other side of the vertex.","answer":"x₁ + a"},"mth-11-04-m06":{"idea":"The latus rectum is the chord through the focus that is perpendicular to the axis. We find its length from the equation.","steps":["Put x = a in y² = 4ax.","y² = 4a², so y = ±2a.","The two end points are (a, 2a ) and (a, −2a ).","The chord length is 2a − (−2a ) = 4a."],"others":"8a would be double the correct length. 2a is only the half chord. a is the focal distance.","trap":"For y² = 8x, the latus rectum is 8.","answer":"4a"},"mth-11-04-m07":{"idea":"An ellipse has a longer axis (major) and a shorter axis (minor). The larger denominator goes with the major axis.","steps":["The equation has the form x²/a² + y²/b² = 1 with a greater than b.","The vertices are (±a, 0) on the x axis.","The ends of the minor axis are (0, ±b ).","Both terms are positive and the right side is 1."],"others":"The version with b² under x and a² under y has its major axis along the y axis. A minus sign gives a hyperbola. A zero on the right side gives only the origin.","trap":"Plus sign and 1 on the right: ellipse.","answer":"x²/a² + y²/b² = 1, with a greater than b"},"mth-11-04-m08":{"idea":"The eccentricity of an ellipse is found from its two semi-axes.","steps":["Here a² = 25 and b² = 16.","e = √(1 − b²/a²) = √(1 − 16/25).","That is √(9/25) = 3/5.","The foci are at (±ae, 0) = (±3, 0)."],"others":"5/3 is the reciprocal and is more than 1, so it cannot be an ellipse. 4/5 uses b/a. 9/25 forgets to take the square root.","trap":"An ellipse has e less than 1, so 5/3 is impossible.","answer":"3/5"},"mth-11-04-m09":{"idea":"This is the property that defines an ellipse. The sum of the distances to the two foci never changes.","steps":["Take the end of the minor axis, (0, b ).","By symmetry, its distance from each focus is the same, and it equals a.","So the sum is a + a = 2a.","The sum is the same for every point, so it equals the major axis 2a."],"others":"2b is the minor axis and 2ae is the distance between the foci. a + b is not a constant property of the ellipse.","trap":"A string of length 2a between two pins draws an ellipse.","answer":"2a, the length of the major axis"},"mth-11-04-m10":{"idea":"A hyperbola looks like an ellipse equation with a minus sign. The sign decides the shape.","steps":["The standard form is x²/a² − y²/b² = 1.","The vertices are (±a, 0), on the transverse axis.","The foci are at (±ae, 0).","The curve opens left and right."],"others":"x²/a² − y²/b² = 0 gives two straight lines, the asymptotes. y²/a² − x²/b² = 1 opens up and down. A plus sign gives an ellipse.","trap":"Minus sign and 1 on the right: hyperbola.","answer":"x²/a² − y²/b² = 1"},"mth-11-04-m11":{"idea":"The eccentricity of a hyperbola is found from a² and b². It is always more than 1.","steps":["Here a² = 9 and b² = 16.","e = √(1 + b²/a²) = √(1 + 16/9).","That is √(25/9) = 5/3.","The foci are at (±5, 0)."],"others":"7/3 comes from a wrong subtraction. 3/5 and 4/3 are less than or close to 1, but hyperbola needs more than 1. 3/5 is also the eccentricity of the ellipse with the same numbers.","trap":"Hyperbola: plus under the root. Ellipse: minus under the root.","answer":"5/3"},"mth-11-04-m12":{"idea":"Asymptotes are the lines the hyperbola approaches far from the centre.","steps":["Replace the 1 on the right by 0: x²/a² − y²/b² = 0.","So y²/b² = x²/a².","Take the square root: y = ±(b/a )x.","These two lines pass through the centre."],"others":"y = ±(b/a ) has no x, so it is not a line through the centre. x = ±a is a pair of vertical lines through the vertices. y = ±(a/b )x has the slope upside down.","trap":"Slope is b/a for x²/a² − y²/b² = 1.","answer":"y = ±(b/a)x"},"mth-11-04-m13":{"idea":"The tangent to a conic at a point follows a replacement rule on the equation.","steps":["Start with y² = 4ax.","Replace y² by yy₁, and replace 2x by x + x₁.","So yy₁ = 2a(x + x₁).","Example: at (2, 4) on y² = 8x, we get 4y = 4(x + 2)."],"others":"yy₁ = a(x + x₁) misses the factor 2. The form with x on the left is the tangent to x² = 4ay. A minus sign x − x₁ is wrong.","trap":"Check the point: put (x₁, y₁) in the tangent. It must satisfy the curve.","answer":"yy₁ = 2a(x + x₁)"},"mth-11-04-m14":{"idea":"We write the tangent at (2, 4) and find where it crosses y = 0.","steps":["The tangent is yy₁ = 2a(x + x₁), with a = 2, x₁ = 2, y₁ = 4.","So 4y = 4(x + 2), which is y = x + 2.","Put y = 0: x = −2.","The point is (−2, 0)."],"others":"(2, 0) is the focus. (4, 0) and (0, 2) do not lie on the line y = x + 2, apart from (0, 2), which is on the y axis, not on the x axis.","trap":"(−2, 0) is as far behind the vertex as the touching point is ahead.","answer":"(−2, 0)"},"mth-11-04-m15":{"idea":"The tangent to an ellipse uses the same replacement rule: x² → xx₁ and y² → yy₁.","steps":["Start with x²/a² + y²/b² = 1.","Replace x² by xx₁ and y² by yy₁.","This gives xx₁/a² + yy₁/b² = 1.","Example: at (3, 3.2) on x²/25 + y²/16 = 1: 3x/25 + 3.2y/16 = 1."],"others":"The version with a² and b² swapped has the wrong denominators. xx₁ + yy₁ = a² + b² is not a tangent. A minus sign gives the tangent of a hyperbola.","trap":"The tangent keeps the signs of the equation.","answer":"xx₁/a² + yy₁/b² = 1"},"mth-11-04-m16":{"idea":"The tangent to a hyperbola follows the same rule. The minus sign stays.","steps":["Start with x²/a² − y²/b² = 1.","Replace x² by xx₁ and y² by yy₁.","This gives xx₁/a² − yy₁/b² = 1.","The minus sign stays, because the equation has it."],"others":"The version with a plus sign is the tangent to an ellipse. The version with a² and b² swapped is wrong. xx₁ − yy₁ = ab has no denominators.","trap":"Same rule for all conics, the signs do not change.","answer":"xx₁/a² − yy₁/b² = 1"},"mth-11-04-m17":{"idea":"A line touches a parabola when it meets it at exactly one point. This needs a zero discriminant.","steps":["Put y = mx + c in y² = 4ax: m²x² + (2mc − 4a )x + c² = 0.","One root means the discriminant is zero: (2mc − 4a )² − 4m²c² = 0.","This simplifies to 16a² − 16amc = 0.","So c = a/m."],"others":"c = 4a/m and c = m/a do not satisfy the discriminant. c = am is a mix up of the two letters.","trap":"For y² = 8x and m = 1: c = 2.","answer":"c = a/m"},"mth-11-04-m18":{"idea":"The same idea, a zero discriminant, gives the tangent condition for an ellipse.","steps":["Put y = mx + c in x²/a² + y²/b² = 1.","This gives a quadratic in x.","Set its discriminant to zero.","The result is c² = a²m² + b²."],"others":"c = am + b is not squared. c² = a²m² − b² is the condition for a hyperbola. c² = a² + b²m² has a and b in the wrong places.","trap":"Ellipse has a plus sign. Hyperbola has a minus sign.","answer":"c² = a²m² + b²"},"mth-11-04-m19":{"idea":"A hyperbola has a minus sign in its equation, so its tangent condition has a minus sign too.","steps":["Put y = mx + c in x²/a² − y²/b² = 1.","Set the discriminant of the quadratic in x to zero.","The result is c² = a²m² − b².","For c to be real, a²m² must be at least b²."],"others":"c² = a²m² + b² is the condition for an ellipse. c² = b²m² − a² has a and b swapped. c = am − b is not squared.","trap":"Mind the order: a²m² first, then minus b².","answer":"c² = a²m² − b²"},"mth-11-04-m20":{"idea":"The latus rectum of an ellipse is the chord through a focus at right angles to the major axis.","steps":["At a focus x = ae, so y² = b²(1 − e²).","Since b² = a²(1 − e²), this gives y² = b⁴/a².","So y = ±b²/a.","The chord is 2b²/a."],"others":"b²/a is only half the chord. 2a²/b has a and b swapped. 2ab is a different quantity.","trap":"Example: x²/25 + y²/16 = 1 gives 32/5 = 6.4.","answer":"2b²/a"},"mth-11-04-m21":{"idea":"The latus rectum of a hyperbola is found the same way as for an ellipse. It is a chord through a focus.","steps":["At a focus x = ae.","Then y² = b²(e² − 1) = b⁴/a².","So y = ±b²/a.","The chord is 2b²/a."],"others":"b²/a is only half the chord. 4a belongs to the parabola. 2a²/b has a and b swapped.","trap":"Example: x²/9 − y²/16 = 1 gives 32/3.","answer":"2b²/a"},"mth-11-04-m22":{"idea":"The ratio of the distance from the focus to the distance from the directrix is the same for every point of a conic.","steps":["Let S be the focus and PM the distance of P from the directrix.","The ratio SP/PM is the same for all points.","This constant ratio is called the eccentricity e.","It decides the shape of the conic."],"others":"The semi-axis is a length of the ellipse or hyperbola. The latus rectum is a chord length. The focal length is the distance from the vertex to the focus.","trap":"e is a ratio, so it has no units.","answer":"the eccentricity"},"mth-11-04-n01":{"idea":"For a parabola, the focal distance of a point equals x₁ + a.","steps":["y² = 8x, so 4a = 8 and a = 2.","The focus is (2, 0).","Focal distance = x₁ + a = 2 + 2.","So it is 4.000."],"trap":"Check: the distance from (2, 0) to (2, 4) is 4.","answer":"4.000"},"mth-11-04-n02":{"idea":"We find the eccentricity of the ellipse from its two semi-axes.","steps":["a² = 25 and b² = 16.","e = √(1 − b²/a²) = √(1 − 16/25).","= √(9/25) = 3/5.","So e = 0.600."],"trap":"An ellipse has e less than 1, so 0.600 is a sensible answer.","answer":"0.600"},"mth-11-04-n03":{"idea":"We find the eccentricity of the hyperbola from a² and b².","steps":["a² = 9 and b² = 16.","e = √(1 + b²/a²) = √(1 + 16/9).","= √(25/9) = 5/3.","So e = 1.667."],"trap":"A hyperbola has e greater than 1, so 1.667 is sensible.","answer":"1.667"},"mth-11-04-n04":{"idea":"We write the tangent to the parabola at (2, 4) and set y = 0.","steps":["The tangent is yy₁ = 2a(x + x₁), with a = 2.","So 4y = 4(x + 2), which gives y = x + 2.","Put y = 0: x = −2.","So p = −2.000."],"trap":"Check with calculus: 2y dy/dx = 8 gives slope 1 at (2, 4).","answer":"-2.000"},"mth-11-04-n05":{"idea":"A line y = mx + c is tangent to y² = 4ax when c = a/m.","steps":["y² = 8x, so a = 2.","m = 1 from y = x + c.","c = a/m = 2/1.","So c = 2.000."],"trap":"Check: the discriminant of x² + (2c − 8)x + c² = 0 is 64 − 32c, zero at c = 2.","answer":"2.000"},"mth-11-04-n06":{"idea":"We use the tangent formula xx₁/a² + yy₁/b² = 1 and put x = 0 to find the y-intercept.","steps":["a² = 25, b² = 16, point (3, 3.2).","The tangent is 3x/25 + 3.2y/16 = 1.","Put x = 0: 0.2y = 1.","So y = 5.000."],"trap":"Check that (3, 3.2) is on the ellipse: 9/25 + 10.24/16 = 0.36 + 0.64 = 1.","answer":"5.000"},"mth-11-04-n07":{"idea":"The tangent condition for an ellipse is c² = a²m² + b². We take the positive c.","steps":["a² = 25, b² = 16, m = 1/2, so m² = 0.25.","c² = 25 × 0.25 + 16 = 6.25 + 16.","c² = 22.25.","c = √22.25 = 4.717."],"trap":"Do not forget to add b², and take only the positive root as asked.","answer":"4.717"},"mth-11-04-n08":{"idea":"The latus rectum of the parabola y² = 4ax has length 4a.","steps":["y² = 8x, so 4a = 8.","The length is 4a, so we put a = 2.","4a = 4 × 2 = 8.","So the length is 8.000."],"trap":"Check: at x = 2, y = ±4, so the chord from (2, −4) to (2, 4) is 8.","answer":"8.000"},"mth-11-04-n09":{"idea":"The latus rectum of a hyperbola has length 2b²/a.","steps":["a² = 9, so a = 3. b² = 16.","Length = 2b²/a = 2 × 16/3.","That is 32/3.","So the length is 10.667."],"trap":"Check: at the focus x = 5, y² = 256/9, so y = ±16/3 and the chord is 32/3.","answer":"10.667"}};
